  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-08",
    "title": "Major Fireball Meteor",
    "explanation": " Explanation:   The sky glows  with soft pinkish colors of fading twilight in this serendipitous mountaintop vista. Taken in subfreezing temperatures, the  thoughtfully composed photo  shows snowy, rugged peaks seen from a mountain pass on 2017 November 14. Below lies the village of  La Villa, Alta Badia  in  Italy 's  Dolomite Alps . Above the nestled village lights, the  constellation Ursa Major  hangs over the northern horizon. But most  stunning  is the intense  fireball  meteor. It was  captured  during the camera's exposure by chance as it flashed east to west across the northern horizon, under Ursa Major's familiar  Big Dipper   asterism . Sightings of  this major fireball meteor  were widely reported in European skies, making it one of the most reported fireball events ever for planet Earth's  American Meteor Society  and the  International Meteor Organization . The meteor's measured track over  Germany  is consistent with its origin near the active  radiant  of November's Northern  Taurid Meteor Shower , a shower associated with dust from  Encke's comet .   Tomorrow's picture:  witch sky?",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/meteor2017_Taylor_1200.jpg?w=0&h=0&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-8-major-fireball-meteor/",
    "credit": " Ollie Taylor ",
    "alt": "A mountainous landscape with snow and trees is seen  below a starry sky. A pink band is visible on the horizon.  Coming down from the sky is a bright meteor trail. Just above the meteor are the stars of the Big Dipper.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-08T08:33:33.952491+00:00"
}